after building an api with apigee and  sharing it with developers there's still  work to be done to make sure that  everyone has a successful experience  as an api producer you may want to know  critical information like how is my api  traffic running over time when is api  response time fastest or slowest and who  are my top developers  the answers to these questions can help  you improve your apis troubleshoot  problems and make better decisions for  your business  this is where apogee analytics comes  into play  apogee analytics collects and calculates  a wealth of information that flows  through api proxies you can visualize  this data in the apogee ui with  pre-defined analytics dashboards as well  as custom reports  apigee analytics can help improve the  api experience for everyone  this data can help provide insight to  the api team about how the api program  is doing overall how individual apis are  doing and how to improve and debug apis  analytics information can also be shared  with app developers who will want to  know how to improve their apps to  increase revenue  the operations team can learn more about  traffic patterns and when to add  back-end resources or make other  adjustments  and the business owner can see whether  or not their initial api investment is  paying off  in other episodes we learned how to  build an api proxy secure apis with api  keys and set up a developer portal  in this episode we'll take a tour of  apogee analytics and learn how can help  you troubleshoot customer issues  [Music]  first visit  apogee.google.com to open the apigee ui  as you can see we've already created  some api proxies and generated some  traffic against them in the left  navigation bar click analyze and then  api metrics  this shows you apogee's pre-built  analytics dashboards for api proxy  performance cache performance error code  analysis latency analysis and target  performance api proxy performance gives  you information about the performance of  your proxy over a period of time  you can see how much overall traffic  you've had including whether it resulted  in success or failure  there's also average response time in  milliseconds  you can also view analytics for a  specific proxy  next up there's cash performance where  you can see the cash hit rate the number  of cash hits and cash response times  you can also take a look at error code  analysis where you can learn about how  many errors occurred and what types of  errors they were  here we have 60 errors that were 404  meaning not found there were also 30  errors that were 401 meaning  unauthorized  these likely happen because the caller  did not provide a valid api key  now let's take a look at latency  analysis  first select a time range of the last  hour  we'll select an api proxy to look at  here we can see some statistics about  response time  at three o'clock in the afternoon the  99th percentile was 97 milliseconds  meaning that 99 of api calls were faster  than 97 milliseconds  we could also see that 95 of api calls  were faster than 14 milliseconds  and of course the median response time  was just two milliseconds  you can analyze response time by the  target as well as request and response  processing latency  there's also a target performance  dashboard to see how fast the backend  system is responding to your requests  this can help you understand when you  might want to use caching  beyond api metrics you can also learn  more about the developers that are using  your apis  under developers you can click developer  engagement  here you can see the total number of  developers the number of developers with  apps and the number of active developers  you can also browse details about  individual applications that developers  have registered using the developer  portal here we have two apps one for  stackdriver monitoring and another for  ping status  for each of these apps we can see the  email of the developer as well as  traffic and error statistics  there's also a traffic composition  section where you can take a look at  your top 10 api proxies by amount of  traffic  in this case the vast majority of api  proxy traffic is for ping status v1  you can also take a look at your top 10  developers by amount of traffic top 10  products by amount of traffic and top 10  apps by amount of traffic  there's also an end users section where  you can see the types of user agents  that are accessing an api proxy  in this case we have some curl calls as  well as some library calls via postman  finally there's a geo map that allows us  to see traffic broken down by country  here we can see that most of our traffic  is coming from the united states but we  also have some traffic from singapore  belgium and brazil  in addition to the pre-built analytics  dashboards you can also create custom  reports  here we have a custom report where we  can see traffic broken down by  application  if you select an application you can  also see the breakdown of traffic by api  call  you can also expand the time frame  beyond the current day  if you want to expand beyond the listed  maximum of 14 days you can submit a job  to handle report generation  asynchronously in the background  you can come back later to see the  results of this custom report  as you can see apogee analytics comes  packed with helpful data visualizations  one key use case for these  visualizations is troubleshooting  data visualization is a powerful way to  unlock compare contrast and assess the  underlying data the apigee collects  about your apis  perhaps you've recently received some  feedback from a customer that one of  your apis is being slow  apogee analytics can help you figure out  exactly what's going on  you can check to see if the slowness is  being seen by just one app or multiple  apps  if it's just being seen by one app there  may be a problem with that app  you can also look to see if the slowness  is concentrated in a certain geolocation  which may suggest a network problem  if neither of these is the case then you  might have a problem with apogee and may  want to look at policy configuration  it may also be helpful to compare the  average response time of different apis  to see if one of them is significantly  underperforming the others  and finally you could generate a custom  report to visualize maximum latency time  by app and resource  thanks for joining us to learn more  about apogee analytics a powerful tool  to help you visualize api data to gain  insights that can help drive business  success  and friends if you found this episode  helpful please click the like button and  subscribe to the channel cheers  you
 

